rvened, holding fixed variables not causally affected.\n4. Compute expected utility.\n5. Choose max.\nInclude caveats: if action changes future evidence, update? Need explain.\n\nNeed discuss \"relevant dependencies\" and counterfactuals:\n- CDT tracks dependencies that would persist under intervention. If variable C causes both A and O, correlation between A and O is spurious for intervention; do(A) holds C fixed. If A causes O, relevant. If A and O share future common effect? Conditioning can induce dependence but CDT avoids? Could mention.\n- If predictor's prediction is caused by prior state that also causes action, action doesn't change prediction; CDT two-box. If action could causally influence prediction (e.g., through time travel or predictor observes commitment), then CDT accounts.\n- Policies: if predictor predicts policy, choosing policy may be evidence but not cause; unless commitment changes prediction.\n\nNeed Death in Damascus: Source says CDT agent unable to process correlation and may irrational. Let's analyze: Death predicts where you'll be and goes there. If choose stay/flee based on predicted location? CDT says neither action changes prediction? If Death reliable, whichever you choose, Death predicted it, so expected death high? If options have equal? If you can randomize? CDT might choose randomly? Need explain: In standard story, Death predicted location; agent sees Death in Damascus? Actually classic: man sees Death in Damascus, flees to Samarra, Death says he had appointment in Samarra. If decision made before prediction? CDT might not know. Source only says CDT unable to process correlation. Could mention as criticism. Better to use Adversarial Offer, as source gives precise. Need calculate: two boxes, can buy one or none, cost $1. Seller predicted buyer not acquire each box; if predicted not acquire, put $3. Accuracy 0.75. Buyer's perspective: For each box, P contains $3? Need seller predicted not acquire for that box. But buyer will choose